.SH DESCRIPTION
An ostrya repository is a directory.
It holds content\-addressed objects, refs that name commits, and a configuration file.
The format is the on\-disk format of an ostree repository.
The \fB\%ostree\fR tool reads the objects that \fB\%ostrya\fR writes, and \fB\%ostrya\fR reads the objects that the tool writes.
Some files and one mode are extensions of ostrya.
The section EXTENSIONS lists them.
.PP
\fB\%ostrya init\fR makes a repository.
The keys of the \fB\%config\fR file are in \fB\%ostrya\-config\fR(1).
In this page, \fB\%<repo>\fR is the repository directory.
.SH MODES
The key \fB\%core.mode\fR of \fB\%config\fR sets the mode of the repository.
\fB\%ostrya init \-\-mode\fR writes the key, and the default mode is \fB\%bare\fR.
The mode controls how a file object is stored.
An object has the same checksum in each mode other than \fB\%bare\-user\-only\fR.
In \fB\%bare\-user\-only\fR an object records no owner, no group, and no extended attributes, and the permission bits of a regular file or a directory are masked with 0755.
The checksum covers this reduced form.
.TP
\fB\%bare\fR
A file object is a regular file or a symbolic link.
Its inode has the owner, the group, the mode, and the extended attributes of the committed file.
A write of a correct owner needs root.
.TP
\fB\%bare\-user\fR
A file object is a regular file that the writing user owns.
The extended attribute \fB\%user.ostreemeta\fR holds the committed owner, group, mode, and extended attributes.
The inode mode of a regular file is the committed permission bits masked with 0775, with the owner read bit set.
A symbolic link is a regular file that holds the target and one NUL byte, with the inode mode 0644.
.TP
\fB\%bare\-user\-only\fR
A file object is a regular file or a symbolic link with no extended attributes.
The owner and the group read back as 0.
The inode mode of a regular file is the committed permission bits masked with 0755.
A commit records the owner, the group, and the mode in this reduced form, so its checksums follow from that form.
.TP
\fB\%bare\-split\-xattrs\fR
A file object is stored as in \fB\%bare\fR, with no extended attributes on the inode.
The extended attributes are in separate \fB\%.file\-xattrs\fR objects.
\fB\%ostrya\fR reads this mode and does not write it.
\fB\%ostrya init\fR refuses it.
.TP
\fB\%archive\fR and \fB\%archive\-z2\fR
A file object is a \fB\%.filez\fR file.
The file holds a header with the file metadata, then the content compressed with raw DEFLATE.
The key \fB\%archive.zlib\-level\fR sets the compression level.
A symbolic link is the header alone.
The inode mode of each object is 0644.
\fB\%ostrya init\fR writes \fB\%archive\fR as \fB\%archive\-z2\fR.
An HTTP server can serve the repository as static files.
.TP
\fB\%bare\-user\-shared\fR
An ostrya extension for development hosts where a group of users share one repository.
A file object has the bytes and the \fB\%user.ostreemeta\fR attribute of \fB\%bare\-user\fR.
The inode mode of each object is 0644, and the committed mode is never applied to the inode.
A checkout copies or reflinks each regular file.
\fB\%checkout \-\-require\-hardlinks\fR refuses a regular file of non-zero length.
The \fB\%ostree\fR tool does not know this mode string and refuses to open the repository.
The section PERMISSIONS gives the modes of the directories and the lock files.
.PP
In each mode, a metadata object is stored uncompressed with the inode mode 0644.
.SH OBJECTS
An object is a file below \fB\%objects/\fR.
Its name is the SHA\-256 checksum of its content, in 64 lowercase hexadecimal characters.
The first two characters name a subdirectory, and the other 62 characters and the suffix name the file:
.PP
.nf
objects/<first 2 hex>/<last 62 hex>.<suffix>
.fi
.PP
The suffix gives the object type:
.TP
\fB\%.commit\fR
A commit: the metadata, the parent, the subject, the body, the time, and the root dirtree and dirmeta checksums.
.TP
\fB\%.dirtree\fR
A directory listing: the names and checksums of the files and of the subdirectories.
.TP
\fB\%.dirmeta\fR
The owner, the group, the mode, and the extended attributes of a directory.
.TP
\fB\%.file\fR
A file object in each mode other than archive.
.TP
\fB\%.filez\fR
A file object in an archive repository.
.TP
\fB\%.commitmeta\fR
The detached metadata of a commit, for example its signatures.
The checksum of a commit does not include it, so a signature can be added after the commit.
.TP
\fB\%.cfs\fR
The composefs image of a commit, an extension of ostrya.
Its name is the checksum of the commit, as for \fB\%.commitmeta\fR.
Its fs\-verity digest is the value of the key \fB\%ostree.composefs.digest.v0\fR of the commit.
Each mode stores it uncompressed, with the inode mode 0644.
\fB\%ostrya commit \-\-generate\-composefs\fR and \fB\%ostrya composefs\-image \-\-add\fR write it, and \fB\%ostrya prune\fR removes it with its commit.
\fB\%ostrya pull\fR, \fB\%ostrya pull\-local\fR, and \fB\%ostrya push\fR copy it with its commit, and install the copy only when its digest is the key.
A static delta to the commit can carry it in a part, and its application installs it only when its digest is the key.
.TP
\fB\%.tombstone\-commit\fR
A marker that records a commit that a prune deleted.
\fB\%ostrya prune\fR writes it when the key \fB\%core.tombstone\-commits\fR is true.
.TP
\fB\%.file\-xattrs\fR and \fB\%.file\-xattrs\-link\fR
The extended attributes of a file object in a \fB\%bare\-split\-xattrs\fR repository.
\fB\%ostrya\fR reads these objects and does not write them.
.PP
The byte layout of each object type is in \fB\%docs/format\-reference.md\fR of the ostrya source tree.
.SH LAYOUT
\fB\%ostrya init\fR makes the directories \fB\%objects\fR, \fB\%tmp\fR, \fB\%tmp/cache\fR, \fB\%refs\fR, \fB\%refs/heads\fR, \fB\%refs/remotes\fR, \fB\%refs/mirrors\fR, \fB\%state\fR, and \fB\%extensions\fR, and the file \fB\%config\fR.
It keeps an existing \fB\%config\fR unchanged.
The other entries below appear when a command first needs them.
.TP
\fB\%<repo>/config\fR
The configuration file, in the key file format.
A new repository gets the group \fB\%[core]\fR with \fB\%repo_version=1\fR, \fB\%mode\fR, and the optional \fB\%collection\-id\fR.
\fB\%ostrya\-config\fR(1) lists the keys.
.TP
\fB\%<repo>/.lock\fR
The repository lock.
Each transaction takes a shared lock, so many commits and pulls can run at the same time.
A prune takes an exclusive lock.
The keys \fB\%core.locking\fR and \fB\%core.lock\-timeout\-secs\fR control it.
.TP
\fB\%<repo>/.update.lock\fR
An ostrya extension.
An exclusive lock that serializes each write of refs, \fB\%config\fR, keyrings, \fB\%summary\fR, \fB\%summary.sig\fR, and \fB\%.commitmeta\fR files.
\fB\%ostrya\fR takes it also when \fB\%core.locking\fR is false.
.TP
\fB\%<repo>/objects/\fR
The object store.
The section OBJECTS gives its layout.
.TP
\fB\%<repo>/refs/heads/<ref>\fR
A local ref.
The file holds the 64 hexadecimal characters of a commit checksum and a newline.
A \fB\%/\fR in the ref name makes a subdirectory.
An alias is a relative symbolic link to another ref.
.TP
\fB\%<repo>/refs/remotes/<remote>/<ref>\fR
A ref that a pull from the remote \fB\%<remote>\fR wrote.
The refspec \fB\%<remote>:<ref>\fR names it.
.TP
\fB\%<repo>/refs/mirrors/<collection\-id>/<ref>\fR
A collection ref.
The summary lists these refs in its collection map.
A directory under \fB\%refs/mirrors\fR whose name is not a collection id is not served and not listed.
A push to the collection \fB\%<collection\-id>\fR writes it, when \fB\%<collection\-id>\fR is not the \fB\%collection\-id\fR of \fB\%[core]\fR.
A push to that own collection id writes \fB\%refs/heads/<ref>\fR.
A pull with \fB\%\-\-collection\fR writes these refs by the same rule: the collection ref, or \fB\%refs/heads/<ref>\fR for the own collection id.
.TP
\fB\%<repo>/state/<checksum>.commitpartial\fR
A marker of a commit that the repository does not hold in full.
A pull writes it before it fetches the objects of the commit, and removes it when the pull completes.
A pull with \fB\%\-\-subpath\fR or \fB\%\-\-commit\-metadata\-only\fR keeps it.
.TP
\fB\%<repo>/tmp/\fR
The staging area.
Each transaction makes a directory \fB\%tmp/staging\-<boot\-id>\-<6 characters>/\fR and a lock file with the same name and the suffix \fB\%\-lock\fR.
A transaction writes its objects there and moves them into \fB\%objects/\fR when it commits.
At the start of each transaction, ostrya removes each staging directory whose lock is free.
It removes a staging directory with no lock file, and each other entry except a staging lock file, when the entry is older than \fB\%core.tmp\-expiry\-secs\fR.
\fB\%tmp/cache/\fR is never removed.
.TP
\fB\%<repo>/extensions/\fR
Reserved.
\fB\%ostrya init\fR makes it empty, and \fB\%ostrya serve\fR serves its content as stored, except \fB\%extensions/ostrya/\fR.
.TP
\fB\%<repo>/extensions/ostrya/summary/\fR
The summaries of the authentication items of the client groups.
\fB\%ostrya summary \-u\fR makes the directory and writes its files.
In the names below, \fB\%<hex>\fR is the SHA\-256 digest, in 64 lowercase hex digits, of the text of an item.
\fB\%ostrya summary \-u\fR removes the files of each item that has no summary, and leaves each other entry.
\fB\%ostrya serve\fR serves no path under \fB\%extensions/ostrya/\fR.
.TP
\fB\%<repo>/extensions/ostrya/summary/<hex>\fR, \fB\%<hex>.sig\fR
The summary of an item, \fB\%anonymous\fR included, and its signatures, in the formats of \fB\%summary\fR and \fB\%summary.sig\fR.
The summary states the mode \fB\%archive\-z2\fR, and in a mode other than archive it names no static delta.
\fB\%ostrya serve\fR and \fB\%ostrya send\fR serve these files as \fB\%summary\fR and \fB\%summary.sig\fR.
.TP
\fB\%<repo>/extensions/ostrya/summary/<hex>.access\fR
The access digest of an item, in 64 lowercase hex digits and a newline.
Each item with a summary has this file, \fB\%anonymous\fR included.
\fB\%ostrya serve\fR and \fB\%ostrya send\fR serve the summary of the item only when the file holds the access digest of the current client groups.
.TP
\fB\%<repo>/deltas/\fR
The static deltas.
\fB\%ostrya static\-delta generate\fR writes them.
The section STATIC DELTAS gives the layout.
.TP
\fB\%<repo>/delta\-indexes/<to 2>/<to rest>.index\fR
The index of the static deltas that end at the commit \fB\%<to>\fR.
\fB\%ostrya static\-delta reindex\fR writes it.
Each entry maps a delta name to the SHA\-256 checksum of its superblock.
.TP
\fB\%<repo>/summary\fR
The root summary: each ref and its commit, the static deltas, and the repository metadata.
It holds each local ref and each collection ref, whatever the client groups, and states the mode of the repository.
\fB\%ostrya summary \-u\fR writes it, and \fB\%ostrya summary \-u \-c\fR leaves it as it is.
\fB\%ostrya summary \-v\fR and a local pull read it.
\fB\%ostrya serve\fR and \fB\%ostrya send\fR never serve it.
A web server that serves the repository directory as files serves it, and with it the name of each ref.
A summary is at most 64 MiB: \fB\%ostrya summary \-u\fR refuses a larger summary, and ostrya refuses to read a larger file.
.TP
\fB\%<repo>/summary.sig\fR
The signatures of the summary.
\fB\%ostrya summary \-u \-\-sign\fR and \fB\%\-\-gpg\-sign\fR write it.
A regeneration of the summary with no key removes it.
\fB\%ostrya summary \-u \-c\fR leaves it as it is.
.TP
\fB\%<repo>/<remote>.trustedkeys.gpg\fR
The trusted GPG keyring of the remote \fB\%<remote>\fR.
\fB\%ostrya remote gpg\-import\fR writes it, and \fB\%ostrya remote delete\fR removes it.
.TP
\fB\%<repo>/gpgkeys.gpg\fR
A keyring that \fB\%ostrya show\fR reads to check a GPG signature, when \fB\%\-\-gpg\-verify\-remote\fR is not given.
No command writes it.
.PP
To change \fB\%config\fR, a ref, \fB\%summary\fR, or a file under \fB\%extensions/ostrya/summary/\fR, ostrya writes a temporary file and renames it over the target.
The temporary file is \fB\%<name>.tmp\-<pid>\-<n>\fR next to the target, or \fB\%tmp/.ostrya\-ref\-*\fR or \fB\%tmp/.ostrya\-meta\-*\fR below \fB\%tmp/\fR.
.SH STATIC DELTAS
A static delta holds the objects that change between two commits, or all the objects of one commit.
Its path uses a modified base64 form of the commit checksums: standard base64 with \fB\%/\fR changed to \fB\%_\fR and no trailing \fB\%=\fR, 43 characters in all.
.TP
\fB\%deltas/<to 2>/<to rest>/\fR
A delta that makes the commit \fB\%<to>\fR from no commit.
\fB\%<to 2>\fR is the first two characters of the base64 form, and \fB\%<to rest>\fR is the other 41.
.TP
\fB\%deltas/<from 2>/<from rest>\-<to>/\fR
A delta from the commit \fB\%<from>\fR to the commit \fB\%<to>\fR.
.PP
A delta directory holds the file \fB\%superblock\fR and the parts \fB\%0\fR, \fB\%1\fR, and up.
The superblock holds the metadata of the delta, the target commit, and the list of the parts.
A signed delta has the same file names, and its superblock is wrapped in a signed envelope.
\fB\%static\-delta generate \-\-inline\fR puts each part into the superblock and writes no part file.
A part starts with one byte that names its compression: 0 for none, or \fB\%x\fR for xz.
A delta to a commit that carries a valid \fB\%ostree.composefs.digest.v0\fR and has a \fB\%.cfs\fR object names the image.
The last part holds the image, or the superblock lists the image as a fallback.
The image is a fallback with \fB\%\-\-inline\fR, at the \fB\%\-\-min\-fallback\-size\fR threshold or larger, and when it is larger than \fB\%\-\-max\-chunk\-size\fR or 128 MiB.
.PP
In the summary and in an index, the name of a delta is the hexadecimal \fB\%<to>\fR or \fB\%<from>\-<to>\fR.
\fB\%ostrya prune\fR removes a delta when its target commit is not in the repository.
.SH PERMISSIONS
In each mode other than \fB\%bare\-user\-shared\fR, ostrya makes a directory with the mode 0775 less the umask.
An \fB\%objects/<xx>/\fR directory and a directory that a ref write makes below \fB\%refs/\fR get 0777 less the umask.
It writes \fB\%config\fR, each ref file, each object, \fB\%summary\fR, \fB\%summary.sig\fR, each file in \fB\%extensions/ostrya/summary/\fR, and each file in \fB\%deltas/\fR and \fB\%delta\-indexes/\fR with the mode 0644.
.PP
In every mode, \fB\%deltas/\fR, \fB\%delta\-indexes/\fR, and each directory below them get 0755 less the umask.
In a \fB\%bare\-user\-shared\fR repository they also take the setgid bit of their parent directory.
.PP
In a \fB\%bare\-user\-shared\fR repository, ostrya sets these modes with fchmod after it makes each entry, so the umask has no effect:
.TP
Directories: 02770
The repository root, if ostrya made it, each directory that \fB\%ostrya init\fR makes, each \fB\%objects/<xx>/\fR directory, each directory below \fB\%refs/\fR, \fB\%tmp/\fR, each staging directory, and \fB\%extensions/\fR, \fB\%extensions/ostrya/\fR, and \fB\%extensions/ostrya/summary/\fR.
The setgid bit makes each new entry take the group of its directory.
.TP
Lock files: 0660
\fB\%.lock\fR, \fB\%.update.lock\fR, and the lock file of each staging directory.
Each member of the group can then take the locks.
.TP
Objects: 0644
Each object and each \fB\%.commitmeta\fR.
.TP
Markers: 0644
Each \fB\%state/<checksum>.commitpartial\fR.
.TP
Summaries: 0644
\fB\%summary\fR, \fB\%summary.sig\fR, and each file in \fB\%extensions/ostrya/summary/\fR.
.PP
An entry that exists keeps its mode and its group.
ostrya sets no group owner.
\fB\%deltas/\fR and \fB\%delta\-indexes/\fR take the modes of the other repository modes and the setgid bit of their parent directory.
.SH SERVED FILES
\fB\%ostrya serve\fR and \fB\%ostrya send\fR read the repository through an archive view.
The view serves these paths:
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\%config\fR, which the view makes and does not read from disk: \fB\%[core]\fR with \fB\%repo_version=1\fR, \fB\%mode=archive\-z2\fR, and the \fB\%collection\-id\fR key of the repository if it is set.
In an archive repository, the view also copies the \fB\%indexed\-deltas\fR key if it is set.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\%refs/\fR and \fB\%extensions/\fR, as stored.
An alias ref is served as its target.
\fB\%extensions/ostrya/\fR is not served.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\%summary\fR and \fB\%summary.sig\fR: the files of the summary of the authentication item of the client, as stored.
This summary states the mode \fB\%archive\-z2\fR.
In a mode other than archive, it names no static delta.
.IP \(bu 2
The metadata objects, as stored.
.IP \(bu 2
The \fB\%.cfs\fR objects, as stored, in each mode.
.IP \(bu 2
The \fB\%.filez\fR objects.
In an archive repository the view serves them as stored, and in another mode it makes them on request.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\%deltas/\fR and \fB\%delta\-indexes/\fR, in an archive repository alone.
.PP
The view refuses \fB\%tmp/\fR, \fB\%state/\fR, each name that starts with \fB\%.\fR, and each other path.
.PP
The client groups of the config give the read rights of each client, as \fB\%ostrya\-config\fR(1) states in "Read access".
A ref under \fB\%refs/heads/\fR or \fB\%refs/mirrors/ID/\fR is served when the group of the client has \fB\%r\fR on its name.
\fB\%refs/remotes/\fR and each other path under \fB\%refs/\fR are not served.
\fB\%summary\fR and \fB\%summary.sig\fR give the files of the item of the client under \fB\%extensions/ostrya/summary/\fR, \fB\%anonymous\fR included.
The view never serves \fB\%summary\fR and \fB\%summary.sig\fR of the repository root.
An item with no summary, and an item whose \fB\%.access\fR file does not hold its access digest, get the answer of an absent path.
Objects, deltas, \fB\%config\fR, and the other files under \fB\%extensions/\fR are served to each identified client.
\fB\%ostrya serve\fR also serves \fB\%/_ostrya/refs/v1\fR, a list of refs that match a pattern, and \fB\%/_ostrya/receive/v1/\fR, the endpoint of a push over HTTP.
These two are not files of the repository.
.SH BUCKET REPOSITORIES
A bucket repository is a repository in archive form in an S3 bucket, at the address \fB\%s3://BUCKET[/PREFIX]\fR.
Each file of the repository is one object of the bucket.
The key of the object is the path of the file under \fB\%PREFIX/\fR.
\fB\%ostrya remote init\fR creates a bucket repository, \fB\%ostrya push\fR writes commits to it, \fB\%ostrya push\-tree\fR writes a directory to it as a new commit, and \fB\%ostrya pull\fR, \fB\%ostrya remote refs\fR, and \fB\%ostrya remote summary\fR read it.
\fB\%ostrya remote fsck\fR checks it, and with \fB\%\-\-delete\fR repairs it.
\fB\%ostrya remote prune\fR deletes the objects that no root reaches.
\fB\%ostrya remote summary \-u\fR writes its summary.
The init, the two pushes, the repair, the prune, and the summary update take the writer lock of the bucket repository.
The prune takes the lock also with \fB\%\-\-no\-prune\fR.
\fB\%ostrya push\-tree\fR makes the commit on the client, with the parent from the ref file of the branch, and signs no commit.
In the ostrya library, the feature \fB\%s3\fR gives the read, the init, the writer lock, the fsck, the prune, and the summary update, and the features \fB\%s3\fR and \fB\%push\fR give the two pushes.
The \fB\%ostrya\fR command built without its feature \fB\%s3\fR refuses an \fB\%s3://\fR address.
.PP
A push that stops before its end can leave an incomplete multipart upload, which holds storage in the bucket.
\fB\%ostrya remote prune\fR aborts each such upload of the repository.
Also set a lifecycle rule \fB\%AbortIncompleteMultipartUpload\fR on the bucket, so that the S3 server aborts such an upload after a number of days.
.PP
These are the keys of a bucket repository:
.TP
\fB\%config\fR
The config of the repository.
The init writes it with the group \fB\%[core]\fR and the keys \fB\%repo_version=1\fR and \fB\%mode=archive\-z2\fR, and with \fB\%collection\-id\fR when the caller gives a collection id, for example with \fB\%ostrya remote init \-\-collection\-id\fR.
With a zlib level, for example with \fB\%ostrya remote init \-\-zlib\-level\fR, the group \fB\%[archive]\fR with the key \fB\%zlib\-level\fR follows.
A bucket repository with a collection id is the primary server of that collection.
The init refuses a bucket that holds a \fB\%config\fR, so the collection id of a bucket repository never changes.
A push, a prune, and a summary update need the \fB\%config\fR, and refuse a mode other than \fB\%archive\-z2\fR and \fB\%archive\fR, a collection id that fails the collection\-id rule, a \fB\%tombstone\-commits\fR or \fB\%indexed\-deltas\fR that is not a boolean, and a \fB\%zlib\-level\fR that is not an integer.
A push builds each \fB\%.filez\fR that it uploads at the \fB\%zlib\-level\fR of this \fB\%config\fR, 6 when the key is absent.
A push of commits from an archive repository uploads each \fB\%.filez\fR as it is stored.
A prune also refuses a \fB\%config\fR that sets \fB\%tombstone\-commits=true\fR in \fB\%[core]\fR or \fB\%gc\-root\-metadata\-keys\fR in \fB\%[ex\-ostrya]\fR.
.TP
\fB\%summary\fR, \fB\%summary.sig\fR
The summary of the repository and its signatures.
\fB\%ostrya remote summary \-u\fR writes them, \fB\%summary\fR first and then \fB\%summary.sig\fR when it has a signing key.
The summary holds the refs of \fB\%refs/heads/\fR, the refs of each \fB\%refs/mirrors/<id>/\fR in its collection map, and each static delta with a superblock.
The update never deletes \fB\%summary.sig\fR, so a bucket repository that holds one refuses an update with no signing key.
Between the two writes, a pull that verifies the summary refuses it.
A push and a prune write no summary, and leave these two keys as they are.
A prune keeps each commit that the summary names, with no parent.
A summary that names a static delta that a prune deleted stays as it is until an update of the summary.
A pull of a named ref reads the ref file when the summary does not list the ref.
A pull of a ref that the summary lists takes the commit of the summary, so after a push moves that ref, the pull takes the old commit until an update of the summary.
A pull with \fB\%\-\-mirror\fR and no named ref, a pull that verifies the signature of the summary, \fB\%ostrya pull \-\-collection\fR, \fB\%ostrya remote refs\fR, and \fB\%ostrya remote summary\fR need a summary.
\fB\%ostrya pull \-\-collection\fR reads the refs of the collection from the summary alone, and skips a bucket repository with no summary.
\fB\%ostrya remote fsck\fR reports a summary that differs from the ref files or from the deltas as a note, and a summary that names a commit or a delta that the bucket does not hold as an error.
.TP
\fB\%objects/<xx>/<62 hex digits>.<ext>\fR
The objects, in lowercase hex.
\fB\%<ext>\fR is \fB\%commit\fR, \fB\%commitmeta\fR, \fB\%dirtree\fR, \fB\%dirmeta\fR, \fB\%filez\fR, \fB\%tombstone\-commit\fR, or \fB\%cfs\fR.
Each other key under \fB\%objects/\fR is a stray key.
\fB\%ostrya remote fsck\fR reports each stray key, and no command deletes one.
\fB\%ostrya remote prune\fR deletes each object that no root reaches, and leaves each \fB\%tombstone\-commit\fR.
.TP
\fB\%refs/heads/<name>\fR
The refs of the repository, and of its own collection.
.TP
\fB\%refs/mirrors/<id>/<name>\fR
The refs of the collection \fB\%<id>\fR, which the repository mirrors.
\fB\%<id>\fR passes the collection\-id rule and is at most 255 bytes.
Each writer refuses the refs of \fB\%refs/mirrors/\fR of the own collection id.
A push reads the ref files of the ref directory that it writes alone, and refuses a key in that directory that is not a ref name.
It does not check the keys under \fB\%refs/\fR outside that directory.
.TP
\fB\%deltas/\fR, \fB\%delta\-indexes/\fR
The static deltas and their index files, in the layout of the section STATIC DELTAS.
\fB\%ostrya remote prune\fR deletes each delta whose target commit is not in the bucket after the prune, the \fB\%superblock\fR first, also a delta with no superblock.
It leaves each key under \fB\%deltas/\fR that is not a key of a bucket repository, and each key under \fB\%delta\-indexes/\fR.
.TP
\fB\%state/<checksum>.commitpartial\fR
The marker of a commit whose tree misses objects, with the one byte \fB\%0x66\fR, in lowercase hex.
\fB\%ostrya remote fsck \-\-delete\fR writes it for each commit that reaches a missing or a bad object of a tree, before it deletes the bad objects, and deletes the marker of each commit whose tree is whole.
A push of a ref whose commit has a marker uploads the objects of the tree that the bucket lacks, and then deletes the marker.
After a repair that failed, run \fB\%ostrya remote fsck \-\-delete\fR again before a push: until then, the bad objects of a commit with a marker can stay in the bucket, and a push uploads no object that the bucket holds.
\fB\%ostrya remote prune\fR deletes the marker of each commit that is not in the bucket after the prune, and keeps each other key under \fB\%state/\fR.
Each other commit object of a bucket repository has its whole tree in the bucket.
.TP
\fB\%extensions/ostrya/lock\fR
The writer lock.
A writer holds it while it changes the repository, and removes it at the end.
A read takes no lock.
The lock is a key file with the group \fB\%[lock]\fR and the keys \fB\%id\fR, \fB\%host\fR, \fB\%pid\fR, \fB\%operation\fR, and \fB\%started\fR.
\fB\%id\fR is 64 lowercase hex digits, and identifies one run of one writer.
\fB\%operation\fR is \fB\%init\fR, \fB\%push\fR, \fB\%prune\fR, \fB\%summary\fR, or \fB\%fsck\fR.
\fB\%started\fR is the time at which the writer took the lock, in seconds since the epoch, UTC.
The lock has no expiry time: a lock that a writer leaves stays until a break of the lock removes it.
The \fB\%ostree\fR tool does not read this key.
.SH EXTENSIONS
These parts of the layout are extensions of ostrya:
.IP \(bu 2
the mode \fB\%bare\-user\-shared\fR and its permissions;
.IP \(bu 2
the object type \fB\%.cfs\fR, the composefs image of a commit;
.IP \(bu 2
the lock file \fB\%.update.lock\fR;
.IP \(bu 2
the directory \fB\%extensions/ostrya/summary/\fR and its files;
.IP \(bu 2
the writer lock \fB\%extensions/ostrya/lock\fR and the markers \fB\%state/<checksum>.commitpartial\fR of a bucket repository;
.IP \(bu 2
the temporary names \fB\%tmp/.ostrya\-ref\-*\fR, \fB\%tmp/.ostrya\-meta\-*\fR, and \fB\%<name>.tmp\-<pid>\-<n>\fR;
.IP \(bu 2
the config groups \fB\%[ex\-ostrya]\fR, \fB\%[ex\-ostrya server]\fR, \fB\%[ex\-ostrya client "NAME"]\fR, \fB\%[ex\-ostrya trust "NAME"]\fR, and \fB\%[ex\-ostrya key "NAME"]\fR;
.IP \(bu 2
the remote keys \fB\%push\-url\fR, \fB\%ssh\-command\fR, \fB\%receive\-command\fR, \fB\%push\-token\-file\fR, \fB\%push\-user\fR, \fB\%pull\-url\fR, \fB\%send\-command\fR, \fB\%s3\-region\fR, \fB\%s3\-endpoint\fR, \fB\%s3\-addressing\fR, and \fB\%s3\-profile\fR.
.PP
The \fB\%ostree\fR tool keeps the extension keys when it writes \fB\%config\fR, and ignores them.
.PP
ostrya does not write these entries of the ostree layout: \fB\%tmp/cache/summaries/\fR, \fB\%uncompressed\-objects\-cache/\fR, \fB\%.payload\-link\fR objects, and \fB\%remotes.d/\fR configuration files.
.SH FILES
ostrya also reads these files outside the repository:
.TP
\fB\%/etc/ostree/remotes.d/<remote>.trustedkeys.gpg\fR
The system keyring of the remote \fB\%<remote>\fR.
.TP
\fB\%/usr/share/ostree/trusted.gpg.d/*.gpg\fR
The global trusted GPG keyrings.
If \fB\%OSTREE_GPG_HOME\fR is set and is not empty, ostrya reads the keyrings in that directory in place of this one.
.TP
\fB\%/etc/ostree/trusted.<engine>\fR, \fB\%/etc/ostree/trusted.<engine>.d/\fR
.TQ
\fB\%/usr/share/ostree/trusted.<engine>\fR, \fB\%/usr/share/ostree/trusted.<engine>.d/\fR
The system trusted keys of the signature engine \fB\%<engine>\fR, which is \fB\%ed25519\fR or \fB\%spki\fR.
.TP
\fB\%/etc/ostree/revoked.<engine>\fR, \fB\%/etc/ostree/revoked.<engine>.d/\fR
.TQ
\fB\%/usr/share/ostree/revoked.<engine>\fR, \fB\%/usr/share/ostree/revoked.<engine>.d/\fR
The system revoked keys of the signature engine \fB\%<engine>\fR.
.TP
\fB\%~/.aws/credentials\fR, \fB\%~/.aws/config\fR
The shared AWS credentials file and config file of a command that reads or writes a bucket repository at an \fB\%s3://\fR address.
The \fB\%ostrya\fR command built without its feature \fB\%s3\fR refuses an \fB\%s3://\fR address, and does not read them.
\fB\%AWS_SHARED_CREDENTIALS_FILE\fR and \fB\%AWS_CONFIG_FILE\fR name other paths.
ostrya reads the section \fB\%[PROFILE]\fR of the credentials file, and the sections \fB\%[default]\fR, \fB\%[profile NAME]\fR, and \fB\%[services NAME]\fR of the config file.
ostrya reads at most 1 MiB of each file, and refuses a larger file.
ostrya does not write these files.
.PP
\fB\%ostrya serve\fR also reads the files that these keys of \fB\%[ex\-ostrya server]\fR name, in the repository directory or outside it:
.TP
\fB\%tls\-cert\fR, \fB\%tls\-key\fR, \fB\%tls\-client\-ca\fR
The PEM certificate chain of the server, its private key, and the CA certificates that verify a client certificate.
A relative path in a key is relative to the repository directory.
An option of \fB\%ostrya serve\fR with the name of a key replaces the key, and a relative path in an option is relative to the current directory.
\fB\%ostrya serve\fR reads each file one time, at start, and refuses a file larger than 1 MiB.
